WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.820
If the gate were the only brake, injected cytochrome c should kill these neurons,

2
00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.820
even in NGF. Does it?

3
00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:16.250
Mohanish Deshmukh tested this directly, with cultured sympathetic neurons.

4
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:22.080
With a fine glass needle, he injected cytochrome c straight into the cytoplasm.

5
00:00:23.083 --> 00:00:24.743
The injection skips the gate.

6
00:00:25.683 --> 00:00:28.163
First, inject neurons kept in NGF.

7
00:00:29.003 --> 00:00:31.403
If the gate is the only brake, what should happen?

8
00:00:37.783 --> 00:00:40.283
They should die. They survived.

9
00:00:41.433 --> 00:00:43.773
Now take NGF away for about a day.

10
00:00:44.453 --> 00:00:46.193
But keep each neuron's own gate shut.

11
00:00:47.167 --> 00:00:52.127
Deshmukh did that by blocking protein synthesis, or by using neurons lacking Bax.

12
00:00:53.087 --> 00:00:54.887
These neurons were alive when injected.

13
00:00:55.867 --> 00:00:58.107
Cytochrome c killed them within hours.

14
00:00:59.047 --> 00:01:00.507
A control injection did not.

15
00:01:01.327 --> 00:01:03.367
A caspase inhibitor prevented the death.

16
00:01:04.317 --> 00:01:09.577
So something below cytochrome c release also changes, when NGF is taken away.

17
00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:12.340
The change builds up gradually.

18
00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:16.840
Over about a day without NGF, more and more neurons respond.

19
00:01:17.933 --> 00:01:21.593
Deshmukh called this acquired susceptibility competence-to-die.

20
00:01:22.583 --> 00:01:23.963
What does the change need?

21
00:01:24.843 --> 00:01:27.023
Look again at how these neurons were kept alive.

22
00:01:28.083 --> 00:01:31.083
They made no new protein, yet they became competent.

23
00:01:31.963 --> 00:01:33.563
So the change needs no new protein.

24
00:01:34.616 --> 00:01:37.216
Neurons lacking Bax became competent too.

25
00:01:38.036 --> 00:01:39.116
So it needs no Bax.

26
00:01:40.016 --> 00:01:43.796
So this change is a separate brake, and deprivation removes it.

27
00:01:44.716 --> 00:01:46.876
Later work identified that brake.

28
00:01:47.636 --> 00:01:49.976
It is a caspase inhibitor called XIAP.

29
00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:54.090
XIAP levels fall during NGF deprivation.

30
00:01:55.283 --> 00:02:00.543
XIAP can hold caspases off, partly because these neurons have little Apaf-1.

31
00:02:01.583 --> 00:02:03.223
Here is a test of that idea.

32
00:02:04.203 --> 00:02:06.683
Keep NGF present, and remove the inhibitor.

33
00:02:07.663 --> 00:02:09.163
What should cytochrome c do then?

34
00:02:15.516 --> 00:02:20.176
Smac is a mitochondrial protein that removes inhibitors from caspases.

35
00:02:21.216 --> 00:02:25.796
Cytochrome c injected along with Smac killed neurons kept in NGF.

36
00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:28.970
Put every result against the two brakes.

37
00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:34.680
A neuron dies by apoptosis only when the gate is open or skipped, and the caspase

38
00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:35.680
brake is off.

39
00:02:36.283 --> 00:02:39.783
Once the caspases run, the dying cell signals to be eaten.

40
00:02:40.663 --> 00:02:45.583
Where this has been studied, caspases expose a lipid, phosphatidylserine, on the

41
00:02:45.583 --> 00:02:46.643
membrane's outer face.

42
00:02:47.523 --> 00:02:48.863
It works as an eat me signal.

43
00:02:49.866 --> 00:02:53.606
Microglia and other phagocytes recognise it, and eat the cell.

44
00:02:54.599 --> 00:02:57.579
The cell is normally eaten before its membrane fails.

45
00:02:58.359 --> 00:03:01.839
So its contents do not spill, and the tissue is not inflamed.

46
00:03:02.783 --> 00:03:07.143
Brake two is on the caspases, and deprivation lifts it without new protein.

47
00:03:08.216 --> 00:03:10.776
This apoptotic route needs both brakes off.
